<!--
  The C# / .NET edition (§31.3, §42 level L2 — Contracts).

    make -C editions/csharp check

  The three subcommands are `roundtrip`, `selfcheck` and `corpus`; the Makefile beside this file spells out
  how each is invoked, because an XML comment cannot contain two consecutive hyphens and so cannot quote a
  dotnet command line at all. (That is the third per-language comment rule this repository's editions have
  run into: a C comment cannot contain the characters that open one, a Java comment cannot contain a unicode
  escape, and an XML comment cannot contain a long option.)

  One project with three subcommands rather than three projects, because .NET allows one entry point per
  assembly and three assemblies sharing a source tree is three project files to keep in step for no reader's
  benefit.

  No PackageReference. §31.4 requires byte-identical output, so the number conversion and the duplicate-key
  rule have to be ours rather than whatever a dependency does this year — which is the same reason the Rust
  workspace has none. It also means `dotnet build` works with no network, which is what lets an edition be
  checked on the machine it is being reviewed on.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Skynet.Os</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>skynet-corpus-csharp</AssemblyName>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>

    <!--
      The gate. §6.1 of the coding standard ranks the compiler second only to making a violation
      unrepresentable, and in an edition whose whole job is byte-identical output a nullable-reference warning
      is a wrong document rather than a style question.
    -->
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!--
      `latest-recommended` rather than `latest-all`. The difference is not strictness about correctness — every
      analyser that reports a defect is on either way — it is that `all` enables the API-design and performance
      advisories, and several of those are wrong about this code on purpose: the value model exposes a mutable
      list because a contract builds documents by appending, and the corpus runner catches broad exceptions
      because finding 164 says a runner that crashes describing a failure hides the failure. Choosing the
      analyser set is a decision; suppressing a rule that found something would not be.
    -->
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>false</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

    <!--
      Culture is not allowed to decide any of this. `InvariantGlobalization` removes ICU entirely, so
      `ToUpper()` and `double.ToString()` cannot pick up a machine's locale — and this edition would not use
      them anyway, because the case tables come from the engine the reference runs on and the number rule is
      §31.4's. The property is here to make a locale-dependent call impossible rather than merely unused.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>

    <!--
      **No floating-point relaxation, stated rather than inherited.** The CLR specification defines `mul` and
      `add` on float64 as separate IEEE-754 operations, so there is no contraction to switch off the way there
      is in C, C++ and Zig. What there *is* on this platform is `System.Math.FusedMultiplyAdd`, which fuses
      when a caller asks for it — so the rule here is that nothing calls it, and `selfcheck` scans the source
      to say so. §31.2 pins the kernel's sine, cosine and square root as a fixed sequence of correctly rounded
      operations, and a fused operation is a different sequence.
    -->
    <ServerGarbageCollection>false</ServerGarbageCollection>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
